[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Repository and Unit of Work Patterns in Data Access Layers"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building enterprise software, maintaining a clean separation of concerns is vital. The Repository and Unit of Work patterns are foundational design patterns used together to abstract and manage the data access layer, decoupling business logic from persistence technologies like Entity Framework or custom database drivers."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The Repository Pattern"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Repository pattern mediates between the domain and data mapping layers. It acts as an in-memory collection of domain objects, allowing your application to query, insert, and delete records without dealing directly with raw SQL queries or database contexts."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Centralizes data query logic to promote code reuse."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides a testable boundary by allowing mock implementations during unit testing."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public interface IRepository<T> where T : class {
    Task<T> GetByIdAsync(int id);
    Task<IEnumerable<T>> GetAllAsync();
    void Add(T entity);
    void Remove(T entity);
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The Unit of Work Pattern"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While a repository handles data operations for a specific entity type, the Unit of Work pattern maintains a list of objects affected by a business transaction and coordinates the writing out of changes. It ensures that multiple repository operations execute under a single database transaction, preserving data integrity."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tracks changes across multiple repositories within a single transaction scope."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensures atomicity: either all changes are committed to the database, or none are."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public interface IUnitOfWork : IDisposable {
    IRepository<User> Users { get; }
    IRepository<Order> Orders { get; }
    Task<int> CompleteAsync();
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Putting It Together in Practice"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a typical application service, you inject the Unit of Work rather than individual repositories. This grants the service access to all necessary data stores while centralizing transaction management into a single Commit or Complete call."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class OrderService {
    private readonly IUnitOfWork _unitOfWork;

    public OrderService(IUnitOfWork unitOfWork) {
        _unitOfWork = unitOfWork;
    }

    public async Task PlaceOrderAsync(Order newOrder) {
        _unitOfWork.Orders.Add(newOrder);
        await _unitOfWork.CompleteAsync();
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using the Repository and Unit of Work patterns together prevents database tightly-coupled code, simplifies unit testing, and guarantees transactional consistency across complex business workflows. However, keep in mind that modern ORMs like Entity Framework Core already implement these patterns internally, so evaluate if the overhead of custom wrappers is necessary for your specific project scale."}]
